    title: "Watch: How Spain's housing crisis has resulted in an early general election",
    summary: "A series of measures to tackle the housing crisis were defeated in parliament on Friday",
    preview: "Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez confirmed Spain will be heading to the polls on 29 November, after Sanchez's Socialist party, which is currently heading a minority government, failed to pass housing reforms in the Spanish Congress. Sanchez faces an uphill battle in his bid for re-election with polls showing Sánchez's Socialist party is trailing the conservative People's Party - which many expect to attempt to form a government with the far-right Vox.",
    body: "Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez confirmed Spain will be heading to the polls on 29 November, after Sanchez's Socialist party, which is currently heading a minority government, failed to pass housing reforms in the Spanish Congress.\n\nSanchez faces an uphill battle in his bid for re-election with polls showing Sánchez's Socialist party is trailing the conservative People's Party - which many expect to attempt to form a government with the far-right Vox.\n\nIn his statement announcing the election, Sanchez warned Spaniards about what he called the \"extreme-right wave\" being seen in other countries and said Spain should continue to be a global example of \"peace, feminism and prosperity\".\n\nBBC correspondent Guy Hedgecoe reports.\n\nFollow the BBC's live coverage following Sanchez's announcement.\n\nRelated topics\n\n- Pedro SanchezUpdates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage.\n\n- SpainUpdates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage.\n\n- EuropeUpdates from your News topics will appear in My News and in a collection on the News homepage.",
